Keep discarding leading audio until a picture has actually been delivered.

Extends 107. Applies on top of it, and the two are meant to be measured
separately: 107 alone closes the hold-queue window, 107 + this one closes both.

107 filters the hold queue at release time, which turned out to cover only part
of the problem. Four recordings per profile, offset between the first audio
packet and the first picture:

    profile      before 107       with 107 (4 runs)        median
    software        534 ms        18, 91, 169, 226 ms      130 ms
    vaapi          1391 ms        269, 283, 430, 1052 ms   356 ms
    qsv            1368 ms        78, 78, 92, 110 ms        85 ms

QSV is consistent and software is much improved, but one VAAPI run came back at
1052 ms -- essentially the pre-patch figure. The cause is the release itself:
gh_passthru is armed as soon as the hold queue drains, and from then on every
packet is forwarded unfiltered. The audio encoder is still running ahead of the
video one, so whatever audio arrives between the release and the first picture
slips out in front of it and re-opens the gap 107 had just closed. How much
depends on how those two moments happen to line up, which is why the spread is
widest on VAAPI, the slowest path to a first frame.

So gh_passthru does not mean "a picture has been delivered". This patch adds
that as a separate condition: leading audio keeps being discarded until a video
packet has actually gone out, in the hold queue or after it. The flag resets on
SMT_START so a re-started stream is treated afresh.

A note on the alternative. Holding the audio instead of discarding it does not
work: holding does not alter timestamps, so releasing it later still hands the
client audio stamped earlier than the first picture and the player still shows
a blank frame. Making it work would mean shifting the audio timestamps forward,
which desynchronises sound from picture. Discarding is the only variant that
closes the gap without touching synchronisation -- at the cost of the leading
audio, which is what makes this a design decision rather than an obvious fix.

Untested at runtime. The measurement to make is the same one: record with curl
and compare the first audio and first video timestamps.

Not introduced by PR #2117: globalheaders.c is untouched by it.
